  "notes": [
    "A lightly oxidized \"green\" or jade oolong, traditionally crafted from the Taiwanese *Si Ji Chun* (\"Four Seasons Spring\") cultivar, prized for producing a vibrant, spring-like floral character.",
    "Features tightly rolled, ball-style whole leaves that expand to several times their dry size as they hydrate and unfurl.",
    "Ideal water temperature is between 185°F and 195°F (85°C to 90°C); avoid rolling boiling water to preserve the delicate floral aromatics without scorching the leaves.",
    "For Western-style brewing, use about 1 rounded teaspoon (2 to 3 grams) per 8 ounces of water and steep for 2 to 3 minutes.",
    "Suited for multiple infusions; the leaves can be re-steeped 3 to 5 times (or more in a gaiwan), extending the steeping time by 30 to 60 seconds per subsequent brew.",
    "Best prepared in a spacious brewing vessel or roomy infuser basket to give the tightly rolled leaves ample space to fully expand."
  ],
